/*
 * attack.c
 *
 * a framework for a basic attack
 * should have three messages, an announcement, a success/fail, and a recovery/chain
 * I'm thinking of something much along these lines:
 * A command file will perform any necessary checks and targeting business
 * it will then load an attack daemon and pass control to that object
 * which will implement an "interface" of the following functions and
 * one public function.
 *
 * The daemon will handle hit/miss/block status, broadcast messages, assign
 * damage, time attacks, and prompt for chains of attacks.
 *
 *
 * necessary functions
 * 
 * set_announce_attacker()
 * set_announce_target()
 * set_announce_room()
 * set_success_failure_attacker()
 * set_success_failure_target()
 * set_success_failure_room()
 * set_recovery_attacker()
 * set_recovery_target()
 * set_recovery_room()
 * set_delay()
 * set_chainable()
 * set_endurance_cost()
 * announce_attack()
 * announce_success_failure()
 * announce_recovery()
 * get_chainable()
 * get_delay()
 * get_chainable()
 * get_endurace_cost()
 * 
 * necessary variables
 * 
 * announce_attacker
 * announce_target
 * announce_room
 * success_failure_attacker
 * success_failure_target
 * success_failure_room
 * recovery_attacker
 * recovery_target
 * recovery_room
 * delay
 * chainable
 * endurance_cost
 *
 * during recovery, partial endurance is recovered, if the attack is chained, only the partial gain from the last 
 * attack is recieved, making chains useful only over very short spans of time.
 * */


